one of southern nevada's most popular  areas for hiking climbing or just  enjoying nature could eventually see  some changes we are talking about calico  basin right near red rock canyon a plan  from the bureau of land management aims  to protect the environment sasha loftis  joins us now with the details denise and  brian a lot of the reason people say  they love calico basin is because it's  free and easy to access those we spoke  with say they're not sure they want to  see that change  we're out here every day almost angela  steffen calls calico basin her sanctuary  and counts on the spot to unwind that's  being kind of our church and our place  to come and  exercise and just get grounded she's one  of many who uses these trails that are  free and open anytime  but the bureau of land management  approved an area management plan this  week that could bring some changes there  are  hundreds and hundreds and hundreds and  hundreds of people that come out and use  this area the goal of the proposal is to  balance a surge in visitors while also  protecting the land fees and a  reservation system could also come into  play  brian friesen president of southern  nevada climbers association says that  could paint a different picture from  what calico basin offers now this is one  of the most easily accessible areas for  climbing there's no fee currently  and you can come out here after work and  enjoy the beautiful scenery he says the  group is still digesting the plan we  need to preserve this land and to many  people it can look  in many different ways but regardless of  what happens  he and stefan hope every person does  their part to protect this refuge  right in our backyard we want to keep it  preserved and have it really beautiful  there is a 30 day appeal period open  before the blm can move forward here if  you'd like to weigh in we have a link on  our website eight news now dot com
 

